  "query_preview": "..(This Afternoon Through Saturday)\nIssued at 1150 AM MDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\nIt has been a day of waiting and seeing if thunderstorms initiate.\nAs of 1PM, one cell was been able to develop in Butte County \nnorth of the Black Hills. Looking ahead, still can't completely \nrule out more isolated storm development near the northern Black \nHills. There are just enough ingredients available but with how \ndry the area is and how warm the atmosphere is, any vertical \nmotion that happens has a lot to overcome.\n\nFor tonight, central SD could see thunderstorms drift in from the\nnorth. Some of those storms could be strong to severe, the SPC has\nplaced a Slight Risk (level 2 of 5) for these forecasted storms.\n\nMonday and Tuesday look similar, with highs mostly in the upper \n80s to 90s. Zonal flow will keep the daily chances for afternoon \nstorms in place over the CWA. SPC has placed a marginal (level 1\nof 5) over the SD counties for both Monday and Tuesday.\n\nThe SPC has also placed a risk of severe weather on Wednesday\ncentered over SW SD. Model forecasts suggest that scattered \nthunderstorms will most likely develop in the afternoon along an \naxis of moderate instability from western Nebraska into \nsouthwestern South Dakota. The storms will be supported by a mid- \nlevel shortwave trough moving across the north-central U.S. \nModerate deep-layer shear near the instability axis should support\na severe threat during the afternoon and evening.\n\nUnsettled pattern under zonal flow will give the area cooler \ntemps and daily chances for rain and storms for the second half of\nthe week.",
